
Wheeled Mobile Robot Simulation Using URDF and PyBullet
Project Overview
This project presents a complete pipeline for modeling, simulating, and controlling a differential drive mobile robot using URDF (Unified Robot Description Format) and the PyBullet physics engine. The robot is designed from scratch and simulated in a realistic virtual environment, enabling experiments in mobile robot kinematics, control, and navigation.
The project covers the following major stages:
1.	Robot modeling using URDF
2.	Loading and visualization in PyBullet
3.	Physical property configuration (friction, damping, gravity)
4.	Motion execution and trajectory generation
This simulation framework is suitable for applications in:
•	Robotics research
•	Reinforcement learning
•	Autonomous navigation
•	Control system design
•	SLAM and perception experiments

System Architecture
The system consists of three main components:
URDF Model  →  PyBullet Physics Engine  →  Control Program
•	URDF Model: Defines the robot structure, geometry, joints, and inertial properties.
•	PyBullet Engine: Handles physics simulation including gravity, collision, and dynamics.
•	Control Program: Sends velocity commands to the wheel joints to generate robot motion.

Robot Description
The robot is a differential drive mobile robot with the following components:
Base Link
•	Shape: Box
•	Dimensions: 0.4 m × 0.3 m × 0.1 m
•	Mass: 5 kg
Drive Wheels
•	Type: Cylindrical
•	Radius: 0.05 m
•	Length: 0.02 m
•	Actuated using continuous joints
Caster Wheel
•	Type: Spherical
•	Radius: 0.05 m
•	Passive support wheel
